== Chapter XII

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[Two inferences drawn from the proposition.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

1\. #smallcaps[It] shows us the necessity of effectual calling. Without it there is no going to heaven. We must be #emph[made fit for the inheritance] (Col 1:12). As God makes heaven fit for us, so he makes us fit for heaven. And what gives this fitness and suitability but effectual calling? A man who remains in the filth and rubbish of nature is no more fit for heaven than a dead man is fit to inherit. The #emph[high calling] is not a thing optional or indifferent; it is as necessary as salvation. Yet, alas, how this one needful thing is neglected! Most men, like the people of #emph[Israel,] wander up and down #emph[to gather straw,] but pay no attention to evidences of their effectual calling.

2\. Take notice what a mighty power God puts forth in calling sinners#footnote[2 #emph[Branch.]]! God so #emph[calls] that he #emph[draws] (John 6:44). Conversion is called a #emph[resurrection] (Rev 20:6): “Blessed … are those who share in the first resurrection!” That is a rising from sin to grace. A man can no more convert himself than a dead man can raise himself. It is called a creation (Col 3:10). To create is above the power of nature.

But the #emph[Arminians] say that the will is not dead but asleep. God by a moral persuasion only wakes us, they say, and then the will can obey God’s call and move of itself to its own conversion#footnote[#emph[Objection.]].

To this I answer: every man is bound in fetters by sin (Acts 8:23)#footnote[#emph[Answer.]]: “I see that you are … captive to iniquity.” If you use arguments with a man who is in fetters and persuade him to walk, is that enough? His fetters must be knocked off and he must be set free before he can walk. So it is with every natural man: he is fettered with corruption. Now the Lord by converting grace must file off his fetters, and indeed give him legs to run as well, or he can never obtain salvation.
